{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794324418,"nonce":"WIgKM6iSoVapsKFZravHDinVYmQ=","message":"Direct backend in the real build (commits c9045efb, 11e5df4e, local): ASH_WASM_DIRECT=1 emits the numeric functions as a relocatable object, skips their LLVM lowering (guard in compile_function; a second path lowers callees transitively), keeps the functions the object calls external, declares every direct function so ash_functions points at it, and links the three objects. Polls use the runtime's exported ash_fiber_poll_epoch through a data relocation, tested inline like LLVM's; shadow frames still call hlp_shadow_push/pop. Results (macOS, wasm32-wasip1-threads, TestWasmBench): fib 76 ms vs LLVM 170, loopSum 527 vs 531, grid 197 vs 188; fiber build: fib 76 vs 172, loopSum 530 vs 415 (LLVM strip-mines its polls, the emitter polls every iteration). A first measurement was invalid because LLVM had also lowered the same functions. Without shadow frames fib is 24 ms: the runtime's push/pop are 55 of the 78 ms. The naive call-per-iteration poll cost loopSum 14 s. Fiber transform: an if with a computed condition around a resume point stopped the link (f71d06c, fixed with a regression test). Conformance, unit suite on the wasm arm (1069 cases, 10971 assertions): LLVM only 1069/1069; with 306 of 8688 functions direct 1069/1069 and 10971/10971; with the fiber transform on as well 1069/1069 and 10971/10971. The harness modules were confirmed byte-identical to deliberate direct builds and different from LLVM-only ones. Coverage is still the numeric subset: 306 of 8688 functions in the unit program, 9 of 356 in the benchmark; most rejects are signatures with object types. Next: objects and the data/runtime contract, SIMD, closures, exceptions, strip-mined polls, inline shadow frames.","files":null}]}